Liquibase Rollback Guide
Overview
Liquibase rollbacks operate on changesets regardless of whether the changeset is defined in XML
or executes an external SQL file via <sqlFile>.
1. Rollback by Tag
Purpose: Roll back everything after a release tag.
Example:
v1.0 -> Customer, Orders
v1.1 -> Procedure, Product
v1.2 -> Email column
Commands:
liquibase tag v1.2
liquibase rollback v1.0
Use for production release rollback.
2. Rollback Count
Purpose: Undo the last N changesets.
Example: rollbackCount 2 removes the last two deployed changesets only.
3. Rollback to Date
Purpose: Restore the database to a specific point in time.
Example:
liquibase rollbackToDate "2026-07-30T09:06:00"
4. Rollback One Changeset
Purpose: Roll back one specific changeset using its ID, author, and changelog file. Useful when a
single object change must be reverted.
5. Rollback One Update
Purpose: Undo only the most recent Liquibase update operation (deployment batch).
6. Rollback SQL
Purpose: Generate the SQL that would be executed during rollback without actually running it.
Example:
liquibase rollbackSQL v1.0
How It Works with the Proposed Architecture
master.xml -> schema.xml -> customer.xml + procedure.xml. Liquibase tracks each changeset
independently. A rollbackCount 2 operation can roll back procedure-001 and customer-002 even
though one is XML and the other executes an external SQL file.
Recommended Enterprise Rollback Strategy
1. Rollback by Tag (production releases)
2. Rollback Count (development/testing)
3. Rollback One Changeset (targeted fixes)
4. Rollback SQL (DBA review)
5. Rollback to Date (point-in-time recovery)
6. Rollback One Update (undo latest deployment)
